WEBVTT

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:03.932 position:50% size:92% align:center
The starter has four input chunks and two

00:00:03.932 --> 00:00:07.834 position:50% size:92% align:center
reusable GPU buffer slots. Copies supply values;

00:00:07.834 --> 00:00:10.435 position:50% size:92% align:center
SMs execute the doubling kernel.

00:00:10.655 --> 00:00:13.483 position:50% size:92% align:center
Separate resources can overlap eligible work,

00:00:13.483 --> 00:00:15.997 position:50% size:92% align:center
but streams express ordering rather than

00:00:15.997 --> 00:00:17.631 position:50% size:92% align:center
reserved hardware engines.

00:00:19.651 --> 00:00:22.519 position:50% size:92% align:center
The copy stream records ready after supplying a

00:00:22.519 --> 00:00:25.387 position:50% size:92% align:center
slot’s input. The compute stream waits for that

00:00:25.387 --> 00:00:27.096 position:50% size:92% align:center
event before using the slot.

00:00:27.316 --> 00:00:30.181 position:50% size:92% align:center
An event marks completion of earlier work in its

00:00:30.181 --> 00:00:32.211 position:50% size:92% align:center
stream; the wait adds a dependency

00:00:32.211 --> 00:00:33.524 position:50% size:92% align:center
from the other stream.

00:00:35.544 --> 00:00:38.473 position:50% size:92% align:center
After the kernel, the compute stream copies the

00:00:38.473 --> 00:00:41.028 position:50% size:92% align:center
result back to the CPU. Only then does it

00:00:41.028 --> 00:00:42.648 position:50% size:92% align:center
record used for that slot.

00:00:42.868 --> 00:00:45.614 position:50% size:92% align:center
Finishing the input copy alone does not make a

00:00:45.614 --> 00:00:48.180 position:50% size:92% align:center
buffer safe for a later chunk to overwrite.

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:53.547 position:50% size:92% align:center
Chunk two wants to reuse slot A from chunk zero.

00:00:53.547 --> 00:00:56.755 position:50% size:92% align:center
The copy engine is available, but the previous

00:00:56.755 --> 00:00:59.544 position:50% size:92% align:center
computation or result copy still uses A.

00:00:59.764 --> 00:01:02.271 position:50% size:92% align:center
Pause and predict whether the next input can

00:01:02.271 --> 00:01:04.095 position:50% size:92% align:center
overwrite that slot immediately.

00:01:09.915 --> 00:01:12.916 position:50% size:92% align:center
It must wait for used from the previous chunk in

00:01:12.916 --> 00:01:15.916 position:50% size:92% align:center
that slot. The next input copy can proceed after

00:01:15.916 --> 00:01:17.542 position:50% size:92% align:center
the kernel and result copy

00:01:17.542 --> 00:01:18.917 position:50% size:92% align:center
have finished using A.

00:01:19.137 --> 00:01:21.628 position:50% size:92% align:center
Slot B permits a different chunk to make

00:01:21.628 --> 00:01:23.683 position:50% size:92% align:center
progress while A is held; it does

00:01:23.683 --> 00:01:25.303 position:50% size:92% align:center
not remove A’s dependency.

00:01:27.323 --> 00:01:29.783 position:50% size:92% align:center
Correct overlap needs readiness before

00:01:29.783 --> 00:01:32.437 position:50% size:92% align:center
consumption and release before reuse. The

00:01:32.437 --> 00:01:34.768 position:50% size:92% align:center
supplied events express those edges.

00:01:34.988 --> 00:01:37.761 position:50% size:92% align:center
Actual overlap depends on the device and

00:01:37.761 --> 00:01:40.743 position:50% size:92% align:center
workload. A diagram of eligible work is not

00:01:40.743 --> 00:01:43.308 position:50% size:92% align:center
evidence of elapsed-time improvement.
